ment extraction, long-running analysis jobs and agentic features are core to where the product is going, and we need people who make these workflows testable and dependable rather than impressive in a demo.<br>Our company size of ~40 people ensures that the best idea wins and political discussions are kept aside. And every line of code helps to improve the carbon footprint of real estate properties.<br><br>Tech stack: TypeScript (NestJS backend, React frontend), Hasura/GraphQL, PostgreSQL (multiple databases incl. PostGIS), queue-based job processing, AWS (ECS / Elastic Beanstalk, S3, SES, Textract), IaC, Claude but all agents welcome</p><br><strong>What you'll own</strong><p><ul><li>Build features across our Hasura GraphQL API, NestJS and Postgres. From schema design to resolver.</li><li>Model complex real estate and ESG domains in relational schemas. We run several Postgres databases side by side (one of them geospatial), and schema decisions here have consequences but also positive impact.</li><li>Own background processing: Excel imports, document extraction, long-running analysis jobs, 3D and image processing. Queues, retries, jobs that spawn other jobs - and figuring out what to do when step 3 of 7 fails at 2am.</li><li>Develop our permission model. Customers range from a single owner to enterprises with organization/portfolio/building-level access.</li><li>Integrate LLMs into backend workflows - especially document and data extraction - and make them reliable, verifyable and testable.</li><li>Debug production. Navigate Grafana dashboards and traces to find out why something is slow or failing, whether in the app, the CI/build pipeline or the infrastructure.</li><li>Spot overgrown or overly complex parts of the codebase and simplify them without being asked. Paying down complexity is part of the job.</li></ul></p><br><strong>What success looks like</strong><p><ul><li>You have shipped features end-to-end across schema, API and background processing that our customers actively use.</li><li>Our async processing (imports, extraction, analysis jobs) is measurably more robust: fewer silent failures, clear retry and recovery paths, and observability that tells us what broke and why.</li><li>You have taken ownership of at least one core area e.g. the permission model, the job orchestration layer or the LLM extraction pipeline and are the person the team goes to for it.</li><li>You have raised the bar on testing and developer experience in the backend, and other engineers ship faster because of you.</li><li>You are a trusted sparring partner for product and the team when we decide what to build and how.</li></ul></p><br><strong>What it takes</strong><p><ul><li><strong>You're strong with Postgres.</strong> Schema design comes naturally, you can read a query plan, and you know when the ORM is lying to you. Geospatial experience is a bonus, not a requirement.</li><li><strong>You're fluent in typed backend TypeScript. </strong>We work in NestJS and GraphQL every day; if your experience is in a comparable stack, that's fine, you'll pick it up fast.</li><li><strong>You've built real queue-based processing. </strong>You designed workers, dealt with retries and partial failures, orchestrated multi-step workflows.</li><li><strong>You've designed or worked with authorization for a multi-tenant product</strong> and know where it might get hairy.</li><li><strong>You'd rather have one integration test that hits a real database schema than ten unit tests mocking everything. </strong>You write the test that catches the broken migration.</li><li><strong>You treat production as a debuggable system. </strong>You can navigate dashboards and traces to figure out why something is slow, and you don't treat infrastructure as someone else's problem.</li><li><strong>You communicate clearly </strong>- with engineers, product and, when needed, directly with customers.</li><li><strong>You speak German at C1 level or better and English fluently. </strong>Our 